-- title:
--   PM ✱1: hypothetical derivations (contexts)
-- statement:
--   **DRAFT** — part of an in-progress public formalization of *Principia Mathematica*; it may still change, and every change is dated in the project's [change log](https://github.com/willmcginnis/principia-mathematica-lean/blob/main/docs/EXPORT_LOG.md).
--
--   Contexts for derivations in PM's system ✱1. For a list of formulas $\Gamma = [h_1,\dots,h_n]$ and a formula $\varphi$,
--
--   $$
--   \mathrm{ctx}(\Gamma,\varphi) = h_1 \supset (h_2 \supset (\cdots \supset (h_n \supset \varphi)))
--   $$
--
--   and $\Gamma \vdash \varphi$ (`Pf Γ φ`) is *defined* as: $\mathrm{ctx}(\Gamma,\varphi)$ is a theorem of ✱1. So a derivation under hypotheses is an ordinary ✱1 theorem. Also: the $n$-th hypothesis of a context.
--
--   This is proof infrastructure, not a part of PM's text: PM argues from hypotheses informally; these definitions let such arguments be written as explicit ✱1 derivations.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The rules over contexts (modus ponens under hypotheses, ∧/≡ introduction and elimination, case split on ✱2·11, …) are theorems; they are copied into the solutions that use them. The repository's two tactic abbreviations are not in this bundle: the platform does not accept macros, so the solutions write out each use.
--
--   Source: [PM.Derived](https://github.com/willmcginnis/principia-mathematica-lean/blob/2e43991fec4221198853c15ba637b6c8430cec4b/PM/Derived.lean).
-- source:
--   Whitehead and Russell, Principia Mathematica, Vol. I (1910), ✱1–✱5; formalization: https://github.com/willmcginnis/principia-mathematica-lean/blob/2e43991fec4221198853c15ba637b6c8430cec4b/PM/Derived.lean

-- PM/Derived.lean at 2e43991fec42, without its theorems (skeleton subtraction);
-- generated by scripts/prove2me/export.py; every command kept is copied verbatim.
import Definitions.Def_PM_core_star1_system
import Definitions.Def_PM_core_statement_formulas

/-!
  Derived rules for the ✱1 Hilbert system. Every rule here is a meta-level Lean lemma of the shape
  `Thm a → … → Thm c` (or a theorem schema), built only from `Thm`'s constructors and the
  already-replayed `t…` theorems of PM.Replay. Nothing is assumed: `#print axioms` is empty.

  Part 1: Hilbert-level rules (syllogism, ∧/≡ introduction and elimination).
  Part 2: PM's chain ✱2·73 → ✱2·77 (Frege's "S" axiom), needed here for the deduction layer.
          PM.Replay2 proves `t2_73 … t2_77` again, line by line after PM.
  Part 3: a natural-deduction layer. `Pf Γ φ` is *defined* as the theorem `Thm (h₁ ⊃ (h₂ ⊃ … ⊃ φ))`,
          so the deduction theorem is definitional (`Pf (Γ ++ [h]) φ` is `Pf Γ (h ⊃ φ)`), and
          `Pf [] φ` is `Thm φ`. The rules lift PM's propositions into a context.
-/
namespace PM
open F

/-! ## Part 1: Hilbert-level rules -/

/-! ## Part 2: PM ✱2·73 – ✱2·77 -/

/-! ## Part 3: natural deduction in a context -/

/-- `ctx [h₁,…,hₙ] φ = h₁ ⊃ (… ⊃ (hₙ ⊃ φ))`. -/
@[reducible] def ctx : List F → F → F
  | [], φ => φ
  | h :: Γ, φ => imp h (ctx Γ φ)

/-- `Pf Γ φ`: φ is a theorem under the hypotheses Γ, i.e. `Thm (ctx Γ φ)`. -/
def Pf (Γ : List F) (φ : F) : Prop := Thm (ctx Γ φ)

namespace Pf

/-- The `n`-th hypothesis of a context (0-based, oldest first). -/
def nth : List F → Nat → F
  | [], _ => .var ""
  | a :: _, 0 => a
  | _ :: l, n + 1 => nth l n

end Pf

end PM


